{"author":{"id":"2eecc7d99b502c158aacb0868dca9358610edc02724d21fe49b0196402d3364e"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1787774753,"nonce":"UNHlbw2Fs/C8iEV3qf3AoTy/0MM=","title":"feat(workspace): fan-in is loud — collisions reported, type conflicts refused, signature order-sensitive (2e1a458)","message":"\u003e Imported from pull request #114 (MERGED)\n\u003e Branch: `feat/2e1a458-loud-fanin` → `main`\n\u003e Created: 2026-08-26T18:43:28Z · Merged: 2026-08-26T18:49:24Z\n\nCloses git-bug 2e1a458. The decided direction: keep last-wins, **make it loud**.\n\nA step with multiple `needs:` gets one `/workspace` built by replaying each parent in declared order — per-path union, last-root-wins per file. That rule was asserted only by code comments citing an ADR that didn't contain it, collisions were **silent**, and the skip signature sorted its inputs while materialization didn't.\n\n- **Collisions are reported.** A manifest fold over all roots (dirs included) runs before any write when there are ≥2 roots: identity is `(blob, kind, mode)`, so a differing exec bit or a file-vs-symlink at one path counts. Every collision gets a provisioning-log line naming path + losing + winning root, and a bounded JSON summary (≤8 samples, ≤1.5KiB, count always kept) rides `/dev/termination-log` — a field the executor already receives and never read. At settle it becomes `EventPayload::WorkspaceInputCollisions`, rendered on the activity rail with a new `warning` category. **Forward-only** (unknown event variants fail replay) — roll forward only.\n- **Type conflicts are refused** (`EXIT_FETCH_INPUT_CONFLICT=5` → `FailureClass::Config`, nothing written). This is **new semantics for one case and a security fix**: today root A's symlink `dist`→X plus root B's dir `dist` silently writes B's files *through the link* — outside the workspace when X is absolute — because `create_dir_all` follows and `safe_join` is lexical. The review then found the **cache-restore leg had the same hole** (`read_dir` follows a symlink); `7510b4e` closes it with `lstat`-first, so the ADR amendment's refusal is true of both legs.\n- **`input_signature` is order-sensitive** (`v2:` prefix, declared order). Order determines the bytes a step reads, so a sorted signature was a latent cache-correctness bug. Compare is plain string equality, so v1 rows read as unequal — one conservative re-run per in-flight rerun on upgrade, never a wrong skip. `cache_key` verified correct as-is (it resolves overlays with `.rev()` before sorting).\n- **The missing test exists**: a real diamond whose branches write the same path with different bytes, driven through the actual binary (`CARGO_BIN_EXE`), asserting which bytes win in **both** declared orders, the termination-log contents, and exit-5-with-nothing-written. Plus a reversed-`needs` server test pinning that declared order reaches launch.\n- **ADR-0007 amended** (licensed for this ticket): per-path union, last-root-in-declared-order wins, dirs union, type conflicts refused, deletions inherently not representable across fan-in, order is the pinned IR's — never completion order.\n\n`Executor::workspace_provisioning()` is required, not defaulted (11 impl sites enumerated in the commit) — a defaulted method on a decorator silently dropped evidence once already (56220d7).\n\nChain: design → audit (11 findings; 2 HIGH — the extraction would have dragged retry policy into the shared lib, and \"this already fails\" was false for symlink-vs-dir) → judge → build → review (MERGE-READY; its MED fixed in-branch). Suites: **987/987 Rust, 106/106 UI**, typecheck clean. Follow-up filed: git-bug b6b8510 (settle-time step-id remap drift in the sample).\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)","files":null}]}